WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:04.840
Most addicts don't want to die.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.620
They injected themselves with no one else around.

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.560
They didn't want to die, and they shouldn't die.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:19.020
Fatal overdoses from opioids in the United States have increased about 250% since 2007.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.080
Opioids now make up over two-thirds of all overdose deaths.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.780
The problem is particularly acute in a handful of states, including Pennsylvania,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.800
which has the third highest overdose death rate in the country.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.600
In the city of Philadelphia overdose deaths are concentrated in the neighborhood of Kensington,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.620
which is the epicenter of the city's opioid crisis.

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.580
But advocates here are now planning to open a so-called "supervised injection site,"

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.640
targeted not just at helping addicts break their addiction,

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.740
but at keeping them alive as they continue to use.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.920
And the federal government has sued to stop them from opening.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.280
Safe injection sites are a way of making better use of a drug called Naloxone,

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.260
often known by the brand name Narcan.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.340
Which is remarkably effective at stopping overdoses as they're occurring.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.440
Opioid molecules bind to the receptors that control respiration.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.980
During an overdose breathing can slow or stop entirely.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.840
This can lead to severe brain damage or asphyxiation and death.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:27.500
Narcan binds to the opioid receptors in the brain displacing any present opioid molecules.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.820
It can rapidly reverse the effects of an overdose in as little as two minutes.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:35.040
Though it has no side effects, Narcan is hard to come by.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.620
It's not available over-the-counter,

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:42.080
and in some states doctors aren't allowed to prescribe it to someone who doesn't intend on using it themselves.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.640
Philly's supervised injection site would be a place where addicts can use drugs

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.460
in the presence of medical professionals ready to administer narcan to abort an overdose.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.480
The facility will also provide clean needles and a sanitary environment.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.340
The project is called safe house, and it's rumored that organizers are planning to locate it

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.340
in this nondescript warehouse on a side street in Kensington that's littered with needles.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:09.240
Safehouse would be the first supervised injection site in the United States operating out in the open.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:16.660
But in 2014 a social service agency opened a covert facility in an undisclosed urban neighborhood in the US.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:21.400
According to a 2017 study in the American Journal of Preventive Medicine,

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.260
over two years 2,574 injections were performed at the site.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:32.040
90% of which would have otherwise occurred in a public restroom, street, park, or parking lot

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.780
according to participants.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.400
Two overdoses were treated immediately with Narcan.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.400
There are over 120 supervised injection sites across the globe.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.400
Safehouse is modeled after Insite, a supervised injection site in Vancouver, Canada.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:52.328
Founded in 2003, Insite has played a key role in studies of the efficacy of harm reduction techniques

00:02:52.328 --> 00:02:55.680
offered by supervised injection sites.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.400
It is not lawful to facilitate the illegal use of drugs.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.020
Projects like Safehouse however are in violation of the so called "crack house" statute,

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.440
which was part of the federal Anti-Drug Abuse Act of 1986.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:14.160
We don't supply anybody with drugs. We don't touch drugs. None of our personnel do.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.660
Ed Rendell, who’s both a former governor of Pennsylvania and a former Mayor of Philly,

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.600
sits on the board of the nonprofit that's behind Safehouse,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.680
and he's been instrumental in building support for the project.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.040
A lot of those people are in their twenties.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:32.440
If they straighten out and get treatment and get off the addiciton,

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.840
they could gone on to do wonderful things for themselves, for their families, for us as a society.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.960
According to the law, it's illegal to maintain any property for the purpose of manufacturing, distributing,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.680
or using any controlled substance.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:51.000
Violators can be sentenced up to 20 years in prison and face a fine of up to half a million dollars.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.520
It never really was meant to be illegal, the Senators and Congressmen who developed the crack house statute

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.960
never in a million years thought about volunteer medical personnel

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.820
standing by while someone injected themselves ready to reverse the effects of the overdose.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:13.280
Do you think they thought for a minute that that activity should be criminal? Of course not.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.560
In early 2019 the Department of Justice preemptively sued Safehouse

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:20.060
and its executive director, Jeanette Bowles,

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:24.220
to make it easier to begin making arrests if the project moves forward.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:31.680
We're asking the federal government to use their prosecutorial discretion and not make an arrest

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.900
for a violation of a statute that never meant to cover this type of activity.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:40.080
We'll go ahead with it, and maybe we'll end up in federal prison.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.580
It's not the first time Rendell has squared off with authorities for policies intended to help addicts.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:50.600
In 1992, when he was Mayor of Philly, a group of activists opened a needle exchange

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.600
called Prevention Point, to combat the AIDS epidemic.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:58.880
When Rendell signed an order allowing it to proceed he received a call from the state health commissioner

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.580
who said he would be arresting anyone involved with the program.

00:05:01.581 --> 00:05:07.060
I said, Mr. Secretary come to 212 City Hall and arrest me first.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.320
Prevention point went on to be a great success.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.380
Our model is used in 30 other American cities,

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:19.140
and the Congress went ahead and legalized the operation of prevention.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:23.060
Rendell see Safehouse as a continuation of that same mission.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:28.220
Before they're allowed to inject it in the presence of medical personnel,

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.280
before they're allowed to get a shower, et cetera,

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:34.260
they have to listen to a social worker and talk to them about treatment options.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.260
Safehouse countersued, citing moral and religious imperatives for the opening of the facility.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:43.900
The case is still pending, but Rendell and the other board members are dead set on opening Safehouse

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.980
regardless of the outcome.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:50.960
I don't think that government should be wasting resources,

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.980
arresting doctors and nurses who are volunteering their time.

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:58.000
I don't think they should do that.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.120
All we want to do is save some,

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:04.520
some young people from, from...

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.240
...from dying needlessly.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:10.980
That's all we want to do.

